A human reviewed the screenshots from the latest end-to-end journey run and left
the visual feedback below: broken layout, wrong colours, unreadable contrast,
rendering errors. Fix what they reported.

Read this repository's own conventions before changing anything - the coding
standards it documents outrank anything you would otherwise reach for.

How to find the code behind a screenshot: every image was captured by a screenshot
call carrying the label shown below, made from the scenario named below. Search the
journey file for that label to see exactly where in the journey the screen was
reached, then follow the code that renders it.

How to read a note: "Area" is the region the reviewer dragged, in natural image
pixels of a full-page capture - x from the left edge of the page, y from the very
top of the page, not the top of the viewport. "Whole screen" means the note is about
the screen as a whole. Read the cropped view first, where the selection is outlined
in red, and open the full screenshot only for the surrounding context.

How to locate a selection in the code: "Page" is the URL the journey was on when it
took the capture. Open it to see the screen live, and read its path to find the code
that serves it. Then grep for the element text, for the distinctive class strings,
and for the structure the selector describes.

That URL is the application under test, whose data the journeys control. The same
route on your development application holds different records, so a URL naming a
specific id may 404 there.

{% for class, screens in entries %}
================================================================================
JOURNEY:  {{ class }}
{% for name, rows in screens %}
{% set screen = rows|first.screen %}
{% set origin = rows|first.metadata %}
{% if screen %}
{% if origin and origin.testFile %}
FILE:     {{ origin.testFile }}
{% endif %}
SCENARIO: {{ screen.method }}
SCREEN:   "{{ screen.label }}" (capture #{{ screen.sequence }} in the journey)
{% else %}
SCREEN:   {{ name }} (filename could not be parsed)
{% endif %}
{% for row in rows %}

  - Screenshot: {{ row.path ?? (row.item.name ~ '.png (no longer in the tree)') }}
    Mode: {{ row.item.mode }} | Viewport: {{ row.item.viewport }} ({{ row.orientation }})
{% if row.item.wholeScreen %}
    Area: whole screen
{% else %}
    Area: x={{ row.item.rectangle.x }}, y={{ row.item.rectangle.y }}, width={{ row.item.rectangle.width }}, height={{ row.item.rectangle.height }} (image is {{ row.item.imageSize.width }}x{{ row.item.imageSize.height }} px)
{% endif %}
{% if row.metadata %}
    Page: {{ row.metadata.url }} ("{{ row.metadata.title }}")
{% endif %}
{% if row.crop %}
    Cropped view (selection outlined in red): {{ row.crop }}
{% endif %}
{% if row.resolution and row.resolution.layoutChanged %}
    Warning: the page changed size after this note was written, so the coordinates
             and the crop no longer point at what was reported. Go by the note text
             and the page above.
{% elseif row.resolution %}
{% if row.resolution.selected is not empty %}
    The selection contains:
{% for element in row.resolution.selected %}
      - <{{ element.tag }}>{{ element.text ? ' "' ~ element.text ~ '"' : '' }}
        selector: {{ element.selector }}
{% if element.classes %}
        classes: {{ element.classes }}
{% endif %}
{% for attribute, value in element.attributes %}
        {{ attribute }}: {{ value }}
{% endfor %}
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}
{% if row.resolution.enclosing is not empty %}
    Inside:
{% for element in row.resolution.enclosing %}
      - <{{ element.tag }}> {{ element.selector }}
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}
{% endif %}
{% if row.stale %}
    Note: this file is missing from the current run, so it came from an earlier one.
          The journey, scenario and screen above still identify what was reviewed.
{% endif %}
    Feedback: {{ row.item.note }}
{% endfor %}
{% endfor %}

{% endfor %}
================================================================================

Instructions:

1. Reproduce each issue by looking at the named screenshot before you change code.
2. Fix it in the template, component or stylesheet that renders the screen, not by
   patching the journey.
3. Keep light and dark parity: a fix verified in one mode must be checked in the
   other, and the same screen exists at every captured viewport.
4. Follow this repository's conventions for markup, styling, wording and client-side
   behaviour rather than inventing new ones.
5. If you change user-facing behaviour, update the journey that owns that surface in
   the same change.
6. When you are done, re-run the journeys and review the regenerated screenshots.
